/*!
    \page qtbridges-swift-index.html
    \title Qt Bridges - Swift
    \brief How to get started with Qt Bridges using Swift.

    \section1 Introduction

    Qt Bridge for Swift is a bridge between Swift and QML, designed to write
    application logic in Swift while using Qt Quick for the UI. Bridging
    mechanism is based on Swift and C++ interoperability.

    Qt Bridge for Swift is intended for Apple developers who want to experiment
    with Qt and/or QML without committing to a full C++ application. The
    repository includes example applications and Xcode templates that
    demonstrate the recommended project structure, how to model data and logic
    in Swift, and how to connect those models to QML views.

    \section1 Supported platforms

    Currently, only \b {macOS (Apple Silicon)} is supported, with plans to
    extend support in the future.

    \section1 Prerequisites

    \list
       \li \b {macOS} 14 or later
       \li \b {Swift 6.0} or later
       \li  \l {https://developer.apple.com/xcode/}{Xcode} or \l {https://github.com/swiftlang/swift-package-manager}{Swift Package Manager}
    \endlist

    \section1 Installing Qt Bridge

    Qt Bridge is distributed as a Swift Package Manager package. You can add it
    to your project as a package dependency using either a local package
    reference or the repository URL.

    \section2 Importing Qt Bridge as a remote package

    \section3 Add via Xcode

    \list 1
        \li In Xcode, select \uicontrol {File} →
        \uicontrol {Add Package Dependencies}.
        \li Enter \l {https://github.com/qt/qtbridge-swift}
        \li Enable Swift-C++ interoperability:
        \list 1
            \li Go to the target's \uicontrol {Build Settings}.
            \li Switch the filter to \uicontrol {All + Combined}.
            \li Search for \uicontrol {Interoperability}.
            \li Under \uicontrol {Swift Compiler - Language}, set
            \uicontrol {C++ and Objective-C Interoperability} mode to \uicontrol
            {C++/Objective-C++}.
        \endlist
    \endlist

    \section3 Add via Package.swift

    \list 1
        \li Specify the Qt Bridge package URL in the dependencies section.
        \li Link \c QtBridge product to your target.
        \li Enable Swift-C++ interoperability in \c swiftSettings with
        .\c {interoperabilityMode(.Cxx)} mode.
    \endlist

    \badcode
    dependencies: [
        .package(url: "https://github.com/qt/qtbridge-swift", exact: "0.1.0-alpha")
    ],
    targets: [
        .target(
            name: "MyApp",
            dependencies: [
                .product(name: "QtBridge", package: "qtbridge-swift")
            ],
            swiftSettings: [
                .interoperabilityMode(.Cxx)
            ]
        )
    ]
    \endcode

    \section2 Importing Qt Bridge as a local package

    Firstly, clone the \l {https://github.com/qt/qtbridge-swift}{Qt Bridge for Swift repo}:

    \badcode
        git clone https://github.com/qt/qtbridge-swift
        cd qtbridge-swift
        git checkout 0.1.0-alpha
    \endcode

    \section3 Add via Xcode

    \list 1
        \li In Xcode, select \uicontrol {File} →
        \uicontrol {Add Package Dependencies}.
        \li Click the *Add Local* button and select the folder that contains
        cloned Qt Bridge repo.
        \li Enable Swift-C++ interoperability:
        \list 1
            \li Go to the target's \uicontrol {Build Settings}.
            \li Switch the filter to \uicontrol {All + Combined}.
            \li Search for \uicontrol {Interoperability}.
            \li Under \uicontrol {Swift Compiler - Language}, set
            \uicontrol {C++ and Objective-C Interoperability} mode to \uicontrol
            {C++/Objective-C++}.
        \endlist
    \endlist

    \section3 Add via Package.swift

    \list 1
        \li Specify a path to the cloned Qt Bridge repo in the dependencies
        section.
        \li Link \c QtBridge product to your target.
        \li Enable Swift-C++ interoperability in \c swiftSettings with
        .\c {interoperabilityMode(.Cxx)} mode.
    \endlist

    \badcode
    dependencies: [
        .package(path: "path/to/qtbridge-swift")
    ],
    targets: [
        .target(
            name: "MyApp",
            dependencies: [
                .product(name: "QtBridge", package: "qtbridge-swift")
            ],
            swiftSettings: [
                .interoperabilityMode(.Cxx)
            ]
        )
    ]
    \endcode

    \section2 Disable Library Validation Entitlement

    When you use a Team in Xcode and enable automatic signing, Xcode may enable the
    \b {Hardened Runtime} for the generated macOS app target. With Hardened Runtime enabled,
    macOS enforces \b {Library Validation}, which restricts the app to loading only system
    libraries and libraries signed with a compatible signature. Qt Bridge loads additional
    Qt frameworks at runtime, and this can cause the app to fail to launch when library
    validation is enabled.

    To fix this:

    \list 1
        \li Open the \uicontrol{Signing & Capabilities} tab.
        \li Select your app target.
        \li Expand \uicontrol{Hardened Runtime}.
        \li Manually chech \uicontrol{Disable Library Validation}.
    \endlist

    Alternatively, you can disable \b {Hardened Runtime} entirely by clicking \b {Delete} next to
    it, but disabling Library Validation alone is sufficient.

    \section1 Running examples

    The \b Examples directory contains simple projects implemented with Qt
    Bridge. For instance, to build and run MinimalApp:

    \badcode
        cd qtbridge-swift/Examples/MinimalApp

        xcodebuild \
        -project MinimalApp.xcodeproj \
        -scheme MinimalApp \
        -destination 'platform=macOS' \
        -derivedDataPath build \
        build

        open build/Build/Products/Debug/MinimalApp.app
    \endcode

    \section1 Using Xcode templates

    The \b Templates directory contains:

    \list
        \li \b {a project template} for starting a new Swift + QML macOS
        application
        \li  \b {file templates} for adding Swift models and QML views
    \endlist

    \section2 Installing the templates into Xcode

    Copy the \c Templates folder into
    \c {~/Library/Developer/Xcode/Templates/}.

    \section2 Creating a new project from the Qt Bridge project template

    \list 1
        \li Open Xcode.
        \li Choose \uicontrol {Create New Project...}
        \li Scroll down to \uicontrol {Qt Bridge} section.
        \li Select the \uicontrol {Qt Bridge} project template and click
        \uicontrol {Next}.
        \li Fill in the template options:
        \list
            \li \uicontrol {Product Name} – the name of the application (also
            used for the target name and bundle name).
            \li \uicontrol {Organization Identifier} – a reverse-DNS identifier
            such as com.example.
            \li \uicontrol {Model Type Name} – the Swift type used as the
            backend model, for example \c {MyModel}.
            \li \uicontrol {QML File Name} – the base name of the initial QML
            file, without the .qml extension (for example main or myView).
            \li \uicontrol {Model Context Name} – the name under which the
            model is exposed to QML, usually in lowerCamelCase (for example
            myModel).
            \li Choose a location for the project and click \uicontrol {Create}.
        \endlist
        \li The generated project will contain:
        \list
            \li an application entry point with \c @main
            \li a Swift model type with the name you provided
            \li a QML file with the chosen base name, already included in the
            app’s bundle resources
            \li a macOS app target with Swift/C++ interoperability enabled
        \endlist
        \li The project template sets up the application structure, but you
        still need to add the Qt Bridge as a package dependency.
    \endlist

    \section2 Running the example application

    The generated project may fail to launch if \b {Hardened Runtime} is enabled
    because Qt Bridge dynamically loads Qt frameworks at runtime. To fix this,
    you need to disable \b {Library Validation}. For detailed instructions, see
    \l {disable-library-validation-entitlement}{Disable Library Validation Entitlement}.

    At this point, the example application is ready to run. You can do so, by pressing
    the \uicontrol {Run} button. The project template configures the basic build settings and
    target configuration. For more advanced configuration, you can adjust
    \uicontrol {Build Settings} in the project navigator.

    \section2 Adding a new file using file templates

    Once the file templates are installed, they appear in the template explorer dialog
    under the Qt Bridge section.

    \list 1
        \li In the project navigator, select where you want to create the new file.
        \li Choose \uicontrol {File} → \uicontrol {New} → \uicontrol {File from Template...}
        \li In the template chooser, scroll down until you find the Qt Bridge group.
        \li Select either the \uicontrol {Backend Model} or \uicontrol {QML View} template
        and click \uicontrol {Next}.
        \li Enter a model name for the Backend Model, or a file name for the QML file.
    \endlist

    The generated Swift file will contain a class with the \c @QtBridgeable macro attached
    and a simple greeting function.
    The generated QML file will contain "Hello from Qt Bridges!" text that will be
    displayed in the app's window.
    Make sure that new QML files are included in the app target’s \uicontrol {Copy Bundle Resources}
    under \uicontrol {Build Phases} so that they are copied into the app bundle when you build.

    \section1 API Overview

    \section2 Classes and Annotations

    \section3 QApp

    In Qt Bridge for Swift, you declare the application's entry point by defining a type
    that conforms to the \c QApp protocol and marking that type with the \c @main attribute.
    QApp provides a default \c { static main() } implementation, which is invoked by the
    Swift runtime at launch to start the application. To conform to QApp, provide
    \c qmlFileName (without .qml) and \c initialProperties, where \c initialProperties
    is a dictionary of QML initial property names mapped to Swift objects that
    are exposed to QML. Only Swift types annotated with \c @QtBridgeable class macro are supported.

    \badcode
    import QtBridge

    @main
    struct MyApp: QApp {
        let qmlFileName: String = "main"
        var initialProperties: [String : QtBridge.QObjectBuildable] = [
            "myModel" : MyModel()
        ]
    }
    \endcode

    \section3 @QtBridgeable

    \c @QtBridgeable is a required type macro for any Swift class that should be exposed to QML.
    Only classes marked with this macro can be bridged into QML.

    When applied, the macro:
    \list
        \li Adds conformance to \c QObjectBuildable
        \li Generates all required integration code
        \li Inspects the class’s public API and exposes supported members to QML
    \endlist

    A property is automatically exposed to QML if it meets all of the following requirements:
    \list
    \li It is public.
    \li It is a stored property.
    \li It is an instance property.
    \li It has an explicitly specified type, and the type is one of the supported types:
        \list
        \li \c Int
        \li \c UInt
        \li \c Double
        \li \c Float
        \li \c String
        \li \c Bool
        \li \c [String]
        \li \c QListModel<T>
        \endlist
    \endlist

    Eligible properties are automatically annotated with \c @QtTracked and become available in QML.

    A method is automatically exposed to QML if it meets all of the following requirements:
    \list
    \li It is public.
    \li It is an instance method.
    \endlist

    To prevent a property or method from being exposed to QML, annotate it with the
    \c @QtIgnored member macro.

    \section3 @QtTracked

    This macro is automatically added to supported properties when the property’s type is explicitly specified.

    To expose a property whose type is another Swift class, \c @QtTracked must be added manually.
    The referenced Swift class must also be marked with the appropriate macro; otherwise, this will
    result in compile-time errors.

    \badcode
    @QtBridgeable
    public class Nested {
        public var name: String = ""
    }

    @QtBridgeable
    public class MyModel {
        @QtTracked public var nestedType: Nested = Nested()
    }
    \endcode

    This macro adds didSet observers to non-constant properties. When a property value changes,
    QML engine is notified automatically.

    \section3 @QtIgnored

    \c @QtIgnored is a member macro used to prevent a property or method from being exposed to QML.

    When applied, the annotated member is excluded from the code generated by \c @QtBridgeable,
    even if it would otherwise be eligible for export.

    \badcode
    @QtBridgeable
    public class Model {
        @QtIgnored
        public var internalId: Int = 42
    }
    \endcode

    \section3 QListModel<T>

    \c QListModel<T> is a Swift container designed to make a list of supported values
    available to QML views, so QML can show the items, read their fields and stay in
    sync when the list changes. When you modify the model through the container
    operations (append, replaceSubrange, remove, etc.), the model emits the appropriate
    change notifications so views update automatically. The item type \c T must be either
    one of the supported value types (\c Int, \c UInt, \c Bool, \c Double, \c Float, \c String, \c [String]),
    or a custom type annotated with \c @QtBridgeable macro.

    Example usage with basic type:

    \badcode
    import QtBridge

    @QtBridgeable
    public class ListModel {
        var list: QListModel<String> = ["test string"]
    }
    \endcode

    Example usage with custom type:
    \badcode
    import QtBridge

    @QtBridgeable
    public class Contact {
        var name: String
        var phoneNo: String

        public init(name: String, phoneNo: String) {
            self.name = name
            self.phoneNo = phoneNo
        }
    }

    @QtBridgeable
    public class PhoneBook {
        public var phoneBook: QListModel<Contact> = []
    }
    \endcode
*/
